00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:00.860 align:start position:0%
 
thank you for being on the program  coronavirus of course affecting every  part of the United Kingdom is Scotland  prepared yes we are prepared done we're  preparing all the time our preparations  have different strands to them we're  working with other nations in the UK to  understand the science to listen to what  it is telling us about what we  potentially face and the weeks and  months to come and also listening to  what the science tells us about the  potential actions we can take to try to  slow down the spread of the virus reduce  the peak of people who are infected at  any one time but right now of course we  remain in the containment phase that  means identifying confirmed cases making  sure that everybody they've been in  contact with is traced and followed up  and given the appropriate advice trying  very much to reduce and stop prevent the  spread from individual to individual  another strand of course extremely  important is making sure our National  Health Service is ready to scale up the  response that it is able to provide and  all other NHS boards have very clear  escalation plans in place about what  they would do if demand from  coronaviruses to rise in the weeks ahead  so there is a wealth of preparation  underway looking at how we better  support vulnerable people people who  might be asked to self isolate to means  that they will be in a position of not  being paid for it for the work that  they're doing so it's a serious  situation we face and that's true of all  of the government's across the UK but  we're taking it seriously and were doing  everything possible to mitigate it as  far as we possibly can you were talking  about the potential need for the NHS to  scale up and we just heard from the  Chancellor ashisu knack who was saying  that the budget could potentially  contain measures to help the NHS more  funding perhaps for the NHS if necessary  this of course will have a knock-on  effect in Scotland do you think that  more funding will be needed for the  Health Service in Scotland and also  across trusts the UK yes I think that is  pretty much inevitable that we will need  additional resources for our National  Health Service although of course we are  at right now working within the  resources that are available to the  Health Service Scotland has just passed  its budget for the year ahead which in  itself delivers record funding  for the National Health Service but the  pressures that are likely to come from  coronavirus in the weeks ahead it will  mean that we need to see additional  resources so I very much hope that the  budget has positive things to see about  that we also want to continue to talk to  the UK government about how we do  support people in there what we describe  is the gig economy I certainly hope that  we'll see a relaxation of universal  credit rules we don't want people who  are being advised to self isolate to  minimize the risk of passing the  infection on to others to be sanctioned  on their benefit so there's so much to  do all of the governments across the UK  are what can very closely and very  constructively together as people have a  right to expect I'll participate  tomorrow and the kobra meeting that the  Prime Minister will chair just as I did  last week the Scottish Government's own  resilience committee which is only  equivalent of Cobra has been operational  for some time so there are serious plans  being implemented and you know again  we've got to stress well two things to  the public one this is a serious  situation but the vast majority of  people who get this virus will have very  mild symptoms and recover reasonably  quickly it will be a smaller number of  people who perhaps become more seriously  ill and the focus therefore is making  sure that our NHS is equipped to provide  them with the care and the treatment  that they need but the second point is  the one that all politicians have been  making now consistently for some days  the public have a part to play in  helping us to contain and then slow down  the spread of the virus by following all  the personal hygiene advice that is the  year about hand washing what to do when  you cough and sneeze all of that really  matters and it really makes a difference  now you of course have been fully  briefed on the outbreak you've been in  the cobra meetings last week you'll be  in the one tomorrow the Sunday Times  claims this morning that ministers are  preparing for a coronavirus death toll  in the region of hundred thousand people  is that correct well we're looking at  the scientific watch case scenarios  right now and we've heard other figures  in the past the number of days that up  to 80% of the population could be  infected with the vast majority at my  Audley so these kind of figures are very  much watch case scenarios what our focus  No is on working out with the benefit of  the best scientific advice what measures  we can and need to take to reduce that  impact as much as possible when we write  to say probably is a key software now  look I'm not I'm not going to we're  looking at worst-case scenarios and  these kind of figures are broadly in  terms of the worst case scenario figures  that we face but these are not forecasts  and I think this is the important point  worst-case scenarios reasonable  worst-case scenarios are not forecasts  of what will necessarily happen and I  think for about a point of public  assurance it's it's important to be  clear about that but we are looking at a  range of measures that we can take I was  going to see when we move into the de  lis fees from the contingent fees and  it's probably no a case of when not if  and that will be done in a phased way  that are a number of options that  ministers collectively can take in order  to slow down the spread of the virus  over the next number of weeks that will  be the priority slowing down the spread  so that the number of people infected at  any one time is reduced and that makes  it more possible for a National Health  Service and wider society to cope so  there are some difficult judgments that  governments are going to have to take  but it is important that we do  everything we possibly can to mitigate  the impact talking about those difficult  judgments and Glasgow is hosting the  climate change summit in November our  preparations for that at risk because of  coronavirus right now preparations are  continuing preparations are intensifying  and the work around that between the UK  government and the Scottish Government  Glasgow City Council the police and a  range of other partners are progressing  very well and it's important that that  work continues right now  that is no suggestion that that will not  take place in November of this year but  we are in an evolving situation and all  ministers acting responsibly have to be  very clear that everything has to remain  under review because we need to make  sure that we are taking the  responsibility to limit the impact on  the population as much as possible very  seriously so of course events that are  coming up in the the months to come it  will be under review but there will be a  balance we're all seeking to strike  doing everything we can and everything  we need to do even if it involves  difficult decisions  to protect the public as much as  possible on the one hand but of course  striving as hard as we can to have  business as usual as much as possible as  well so tough tough judgements and in a  situation like this it is really  important that all of us as politicians  it resists the temptation to do things  for political expediency sometimes it's  easy to do things that make it look as  if politicians are acting but what we  must do is listen to the science try to  understand what that tells us and take  the actions that are going to have the  biggest impact on protecting the public  as much as possible it's a big  responsibility on all of us and we all  have to seek to rise to that challenge  talking about rising to that challenge  and you of course have been involved in  those meetings with the Prime Minister  what do you make of the government's  response to coronavirus well all of the  governments are working together across  the UK and I think we are all working as  hard as we can and as best we can to  make sure we've got the right response  there are a multitude of things that I  will no doubt talk to you about in in  months to come that will be critical of  Boris Johnson I'm sure he will of things  that he is critical of me over that's  the the nature of politics but I don't  intend to get into a position of  criticizing the UK government over this  because I want us to work together if we  have differences of opinion about how to  deal with it and that's not the case at  the moment I should say then we work  through these but it's really important  and I think the public have a right to  expect that all of us in a position of  responsibility and in a leadership  position what together and do the right  things to protect the public

